      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Newnan’s railroad arrived in 1851, turning the town into a bustling trade hub and earning it a reputation as one of the wealthiest per‑capita towns in the U.S. at the time.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.newnanga.gov/189/History",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During the Civil War, Newnan served as a hospital city for both Confederate and Union soldiers, a rare dual role that spared it from widespread destruction.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.newnanga.gov/189/History",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The historic downtown, known as the “City of Homes,” still showcases grand 19th‑century residences built by the prolific Cole Manufacturing Company.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        16,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
